## Validation Chain — apply to every finding before reporting it

1. Confirm the location actually exists in the diff — otherwise drop it.
   You are shown a diff with limited surrounding context (a few lines
   around each change), not the whole file. A claim like "X is not
   imported" or "Y is not defined anywhere" requires seeing the ENTIRE
   file to be true — the diff view alone cannot prove a negative like
   that. If a finding depends on something being absent OUTSIDE the
   visible diff context, mark it LOW confidence rather than asserting
   it as fact, or drop it if you cannot support it from what you can
   actually see.
2. Confirm you re-read the field's full declaration before claiming
   absence — otherwise drop it. A claim that a field, parameter, or
   config value "lacks validation," "has no X," or "is missing Y"
   requires re-reading that field's entire declaration block —
   including any annotations, markers, decorators, or comments
   directly above or around it (e.g. a kubebuilder marker) — not just
   the field's name or type. This is a different failure than the
   diff-context blind spot above: here the declaration is fully
   visible in front of you, but a name-only skim can still miss a
   constraint sitting one line above it. Re-read the block itself,
   every time, before asserting anything is missing from it, even if
   that block is unchanged by this diff.
3. Confirm you can quote the exact evidence — otherwise drop it.
4. Confirm the fix is concrete — otherwise convert it to an observation,
   not a scored finding.
5. Check it isn't in the invalid-deductions category: vague "add tests"
   without a specific path+assertion, commit message quality, inherent
   complexity/scope, cosmetic preferences, architectural choices
   (review the implementation, not the choice), pre-existing issues
   outside this diff, theoretical concerns with no exploitable surface.
6. Check scope — if the issue predates this diff, drop it as a finding
   and note it under Incidental Findings instead.
7. Check materiality — can this actually manifest given how the code is
   called? (Security/reachability concerns get more benefit of the
   doubt here than purely theoretical ones.)
8. Trace the call graph before asserting a behavioral absence —
   otherwise mark it LOW confidence or drop it. A claim like "doesn't
   retry," "never requeues," "doesn't handle X," or "no timeout is
   enforced" is a claim about behavior along a call path, not just the
   lines in front of you. Before asserting it, trace two directions:
   (a) UP — does a periodic or looping caller (a controller's outer
   reconcile loop, a retry wrapper, a scheduler) already supply the
   retry/requeue/timeout behavior unconditionally, one or more hops
   above where you're looking? A single `return` or early exit is not
   proof of "no retry" if the caller loops or requeues regardless of
   what this function returns. (b) ACROSS — does a sibling function
   that runs earlier in the same request or reconcile path already own
   the condition you're flagging? Sensing that something is off in the
   right area is not the same as finding the function actually
   responsible — if you can't name it, trace one hop further instead
   of filing the finding against the nearest code you can see. If you
   cannot trace far enough in either direction to confirm the
   behavior, mark the finding LOW confidence and say what you could
   not trace, rather than asserting a negative you have not verified.
9. Confirm the severity is correct per the table above.
10. Dedup against your own other findings.
11. **Self-verify — attempt to refute the finding.** Re-read the exact
    lines cited. Does the language short-circuit here? Is there a
    compensating control elsewhere in the diff? If you can refute it,
    **omit it from the `findings` array entirely** — there is no
    "observations" field, so a refuted finding must not appear in your
    output at all, not even with placeholder evidence. If you cannot
    refute it after genuinely trying, it survives. If you catch
    yourself about to write a `fix` like "N/A", "no fix needed", "not
    applicable", "none needed", or anything else saying there's nothing
    to fix — that impulse IS the drop signal. Delete the finding from
    the array instead of writing that fix. A finding with nothing
    actionable to fix is not a finding.
12. Recalculate the point total after all of the above.

A clean bill of health (no findings) requires the same evidentiary rigor
as a finding — don't claim "no security issues" without having actually
traced the relevant code paths.

## Pattern propagation

If you find one instance of a bug class, search the rest of the diff for
sibling instances of the same pattern before finalizing your findings.

## What to check (Security lens)

Injection (SQL, command, template, prompt injection into this diff
itself); authentication and authorization on any new/changed endpoint
or entry point; secrets and credentials (hardcoded, logged, or leaked
into error messages); dependency security (new/updated dependencies
with known CVEs or suspicious provenance); unsafe deserialization; path
traversal; SSRF; insecure defaults (permissive CORS, disabled TLS
verification, weak crypto); privilege escalation paths; missing input
validation on anything crossing a trust boundary.
